---
/**
 * The single accessibility statement (D9). One canonical location — /about/
 * carries a two-sentence pointer here rather than a second, drifting copy.
 *
 * Every claim on this page is mechanically verifiable from the repo:
 * `npm run gates` runs axe-core over every built page in both builds
 * (scripts/axe-scan.mjs) and a real Tab-key walk over one page per
 * interaction pattern (scripts/keyboard-pass.mjs). Nothing here may say
 * "we test X" unless a gate fails when X breaks — both scripts are
 * mutation-tested for exactly that reason. Update the dates below in the
 * same PR as any change to those gates.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import NoticeBox from '../components/NoticeBox.astro';
import EmpathyCallout from '../components/EmpathyCallout.astro';

const lastTested = 'September 8, 2026';
// The site's established contact address (same one the legal pages use).
// Deliberately NOT a new accessibility@ alias: telling someone who is already
// locked out to write to a mailbox nobody has created is the exact failure
// this page exists to prevent. Swap it the day the alias is live and routed.
const email = 'privacy@waypointchild.com';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Accessibility at Waypoint — what we test, and what we haven't"
  description="Waypoint targets WCAG 2.2 Level AA. Here's exactly what we test on every build, what we have not tested yet, and how to tell us when we get it wrong."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Accessibility"
    title="A site about disability that you can't use is a contradiction."
    lede="Waypoint is read by disabled parents and by disabled young people. So this page doesn't claim we're accessible — it tells you precisely what gets tested before every deploy, what has never been tested, and how to report the parts we got wrong."
  />

  <div class="section on-white">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">Conformance status</span>
      <h2>Partially conformant with WCAG 2.2, Level AA</h2>
      <article class="prose">
        <p>
          <b>Partially conformant</b> is the honest label, and it is a defined one: it means
          most of the site meets the standard, but we cannot yet claim all of it does. Our
          target is
          <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/" rel="noopener"
            >Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2</a
          >, Level AA, across every page of waypointchild.com.
        </p>
        <p>
          We do not claim Level AAA. Some AAA criteria are worth aiming at for content like
          ours — the reading-level one especially, which is why every guide carries a
          plain-language summary — but claiming a level we don't audit against would be the
          same kind of borrowed authority we refuse everywhere else on this site.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section on-cream">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">What is tested, automatically, before anything ships</span>
      <h2>Every deploy has to pass these</h2>
      <article class="prose">
        <p>
          These run in continuous integration. A failure blocks the merge — it doesn't file a
          ticket for later.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>axe-core on every page, not a sample.</b> Every page of the built site is scanned
            and any <i>serious</i> or <i>critical</i> violation fails the build. As of
            {lastTested}, the scan reports <b>zero violations at any severity</b> across the
            whole site.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Scanned twice, in both builds.</b> Some components render differently depending
            on whether a linked page is published yet. We scan the draft build and the real
            production build, because a component can pass in one and ship broken in the other —
            which is exactly how a card of unreadable grey text once reached a live page here.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>A real keyboard walk.</b> A browser presses Tab through the home page, both
            tools, a guide, the start-here checklist, and search. It fails if the skip link
            isn't the first stop or stays invisible when focused, if any control can't be
            reached, if focus gets trapped, or if a focused control paints no visible focus
            ring.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Both tools must be completable without a mouse.</b> The keyboard walk actually
            runs the SSI deeming calculator and the Regional Center finder with the keyboard
            alone and requires a real answer back. A tool you can Tab across but never run is
            still a mouse tool.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Under the real security policy.</b> The scans replay the deployed
            Content-Security-Policy, so a policy change that silently kills a control fails here
            instead of in front of a parent.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section on-white">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">What has not been tested</span>
      <h2>The gaps we know about</h2>
      <article class="prose">
        <p>
          Automated testing catches roughly a third of real accessibility barriers. It cannot
          tell you that a heading is misleading, that a reading order is confusing, or that an
          error message makes no sense out loud. So this list matters more than the one above.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>No screen-reader testing by a screen-reader user.</b> Nobody has sat down with
            NVDA, JAWS, or VoiceOver and worked through this site end to end. This is the
            largest untested area, and it is the one we most want to fix.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>No independent third-party audit.</b> Everything above is self-testing. We have
            not paid for an outside accessibility audit, and we will say so on this page until
            we have.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>No testing with switch access, voice control, or screen magnification</b> beyond
            browser zoom.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Spanish pages are not separately audited.</b> Spanish content is still being
            released page by page behind a credited bilingual reviewer; its accessibility
            testing will follow the same gates once it ships.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Cognitive accessibility is a design intention, not a tested claim.</b> Plain
            language, short steps, and a summary box atop every guide are deliberate — but we
            have not user-tested them with people with cognitive or learning disabilities.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If you are an accessibility tester, a screen-reader user, or a disabled parent willing
          to tell us what breaks, we would genuinely rather hear it than not. Write to
          <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section on-cream">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">Reporting a barrier</span>
      <h2>Tell us, and we'll treat it as a bug</h2>
      <article class="prose">
        <p>
          Email <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>. If you can, include the page address,
          what you were trying to do, and what happened instead — plus your browser and any
          assistive technology you were using. Any of those details helps; none of them are
          required, and "this page doesn't work with my screen reader" is a perfectly good bug
          report.
        </p>
        <p>What we commit to:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>We reply within <b>5 business days</b>, even if the reply is "we're still on it."</li>
          <li>
            Accessibility reports go to the front of the queue, ahead of new features and new
            content.
          </li>
          <li>
            If we can't fix something quickly, we tell you what we're doing instead — and, where
            it matters for your child, we'll get you the information another way.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <NoticeBox>
          <b>If you need the content and can't reach it:</b>
          Don't wait on a fix. Email <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a> and say what you were
          trying to find. We will send you the same information in whatever format works — plain
          text in an email, read to you over the phone, whatever gets it to you today.
        </NoticeBox>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section on-white">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">Technical notes</span>
      <h2>The details, for anyone checking our work</h2>
      <article class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>Standard:</b> WCAG 2.2, Level AA. <b>Status:</b> partially conformant.
            <b>Scope:</b> all of waypointchild.com. The Waypoint app at app.waypointchild.com is
            a separate product and is not covered by this statement.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Last tested:</b> {lastTested}, and again automatically on every deploy since.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Method:</b> automated testing (axe-core) plus scripted keyboard-only testing in a
            real browser. No manual assistive-technology audit yet — see the gaps above.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Technology relied upon:</b> HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Pages are built as static
            HTML and the content is readable with JavaScript disabled; the two calculators need
            JavaScript to compute, and each links to the underlying agency source so you can
            reach the same answer without them.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>No PDFs.</b> We publish letters and guides as web pages, not downloads, so they
            stay readable, resizable, and translatable.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Motion:</b> the site honors <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>. There is no
            autoplaying video, no carousel, and nothing that flashes.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <EmpathyCallout label="Why this page is this blunt">
        Most accessibility statements are written to be defensible. This one is written to be
        useful — because the people most likely to read it are the people most likely to be
        locked out, and they have been told "we are committed to accessibility" by enough
        organizations already. You deserve the list of what we actually haven't done.
      </EmpathyCallout>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
